Well, they consider humans to be just "humans". It's not
so much a Real Life taxonomy. The source code comments say

  @   humans, including elves and were-critters
  h   humanoids

And as a simple rule, @ generally don't respect Elbereth.
(Also minotauri and A [Angelic beings] don't respect it.)

> In my experience, everything on the astral plane is rather
> seriously underpowered compared to the Wizard of Yendor.
> It's anticlimactic, if Rodney doesn't show up on Astral.

I disagree on that. The priests and player characters can
deal a lot of damage - some carry even critical artifact
weapons - and they can easily surround you which multiplies
the damage, and the special effects of the Riders complicate
matters. You need some good preparation there; best, IME,
are wands of teleport, spell of jumping, and wands of death.
Highly enchanted multi-fire missile weapons are also good,
specifically to deal with the Riders.

> The first time I got to the astral plane, I intended to do a
> comparison of the riders, to see whether I agree with the
> conventional wisdom as to which one is the "big threat".
> I went to all three altars and killed all three of the riders
> at least twice each, but I was unable to discern any real
> difference between them in terms of difficulty.  I'd have
> stuck around longer and played with them some more
> (they weren't scaring me at all), but Rodney would have
> showed up eventually, and I *was* a little afraid of him,
> so I went ahead and finished up at that point.

You can prepare yourself so that the Riders are not much of a
threat. Their HP's are usually not too high, and two of them
are susceptible to the wand of death effect (unless you don't
miss, of course). The hassle begins if the riders can get and
act in melee range.

